WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:14.735
Hi, I'm Mike LaBruno,

00:00:14.735 --> 00:00:17.200
owner of All-Brite
Pressure Washing &amp; More.

00:00:17.200 --> 00:00:18.783
We specialize in gutter cleaning

00:00:18.783 --> 00:00:22.066
and pressure washing
of homes and businesses

00:00:22.066 --> 00:00:23.923
in southeast North Carolina.

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:29.247
I'm going to show you how we clean

00:00:29.247 --> 00:00:31.066
clogged gutters before winter.

00:00:33.190 --> 00:00:34.207
In the fall, we're going to have

00:00:34.207 --> 00:00:36.527
a lot of leaves and debris that fall,

00:00:36.527 --> 00:00:38.135
so it's important to check your gutters

00:00:38.135 --> 00:00:39.876
before a snowstorm.

00:00:39.876 --> 00:00:42.830
If we have snow that
accumulates on a roof,

00:00:42.830 --> 00:00:44.550
we can have some water runoff.

00:00:44.550 --> 00:00:47.340
As temperatures rise, that snow will melt,

00:00:47.340 --> 00:00:50.910
causing excessive rainwater
to flow into the gutter

00:00:50.910 --> 00:00:53.450
and freeze and unfreeze,

00:00:53.450 --> 00:00:56.070
potentially causing water to expand

00:00:56.070 --> 00:00:58.670
back up into the interior of your home

00:00:58.670 --> 00:01:01.940
underneath the roofline,

00:01:08.227 --> 00:01:10.070
We start by inspecting gutters

00:01:10.070 --> 00:01:12.780
by removing any debris from the roof.

00:01:12.780 --> 00:01:16.205
Dry debris consists of leaves, dirt,

00:01:16.205 --> 00:01:18.129
and shingle grit, which can cause

00:01:18.129 --> 00:01:20.550
excessive weight inside of the gutter too.

00:01:25.980 --> 00:01:27.804
A pressure washer is a great tool

00:01:27.804 --> 00:01:29.579
for cleaning out gutters,

00:01:29.579 --> 00:01:31.360
but if it's too compacted,

00:01:31.360 --> 00:01:34.390
we will first need to
resort to handpicking this.

00:01:34.390 --> 00:01:37.560
We want to pick out as
many clumps and clogs,

00:01:37.560 --> 00:01:40.500
wet or dry debris, as we can.

00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:43.170
Wet debris is just a little bit muddier,

00:01:43.170 --> 00:01:45.260
and we have water added with it,

00:01:45.260 --> 00:01:47.992
which is excessive weight
inside of the gutter,

00:01:47.992 --> 00:01:49.840
stressing it out.

00:01:49.840 --> 00:01:51.856
If we find a big clog,

00:01:51.856 --> 00:01:54.240
such as wet compacted debris,

00:01:54.240 --> 00:01:57.290
in some cases, we have to
take the downspout apart.

00:01:57.290 --> 00:01:58.560
We will start at the bottom

00:01:58.560 --> 00:02:00.950
by removing rivets or screws

00:02:00.950 --> 00:02:04.467
to get closer to our clog or backup.

00:02:05.392 --> 00:02:08.010
Once we're done handpicking
all the debris we can,

00:02:08.010 --> 00:02:10.290
we want to perform our flush test.

00:02:10.290 --> 00:02:12.330
This is where we will
take a pressure washer

00:02:12.330 --> 00:02:15.130
and run water in the top of the gutter

00:02:15.130 --> 00:02:16.600
towards the downspout

00:02:16.600 --> 00:02:19.140
for at least 10 to 20 seconds.

00:02:19.140 --> 00:02:21.190
We want to remove any remaining debris

00:02:21.190 --> 00:02:23.960
that we did not get from handpicking,

00:02:23.960 --> 00:02:27.210
and we want to see good,
clear flow of water

00:02:27.210 --> 00:02:30.060
diverting away from the
home and foundation.

00:02:30.060 --> 00:02:31.718
We will start at the top of the gutter

00:02:31.718 --> 00:02:33.965
and then move to the downspout

00:02:33.965 --> 00:02:36.800
to ensure we have no remaining debris

00:02:36.800 --> 00:02:38.975
and everything is flowing correctly.

00:02:43.290 --> 00:02:46.281
If we see clear water and good flow

00:02:46.281 --> 00:02:47.806
and hear a hollow sound,

00:02:49.624 --> 00:02:51.190
there are no clogs present.

00:02:51.190 --> 00:02:52.670
If there is a clog present,

00:02:52.670 --> 00:02:55.410
we want to tap on that downspout

00:02:55.410 --> 00:02:58.190
and determine where the clog is.

00:02:58.190 --> 00:02:59.273
We want to run water

00:02:59.273 --> 00:03:01.514
through the top of the downspout

00:03:01.514 --> 00:03:03.520
and have someone at the bottom

00:03:03.520 --> 00:03:06.010
checking to make sure that debris

00:03:06.010 --> 00:03:08.480
is starting to flow through.

00:03:08.480 --> 00:03:10.555
Here, this is all pine straw

00:03:10.555 --> 00:03:13.380
that's been collecting for
at least a couple years.

00:03:13.380 --> 00:03:15.500
We are using a tap-shake method

00:03:15.500 --> 00:03:18.300
along with running water
in the top of the gutter

00:03:18.300 --> 00:03:19.712
to break up the debris

00:03:19.712 --> 00:03:22.113
and flush out all of the pine straw.

00:03:25.420 --> 00:03:26.253
A good way to tell

00:03:26.253 --> 00:03:28.036
that your gutters are flowing properly

00:03:28.036 --> 00:03:30.052
is there will be no water running

00:03:30.052 --> 00:03:31.593
over the top of the gutter

00:03:31.593 --> 00:03:33.284
and you will see clear water

00:03:33.284 --> 00:03:35.430
at the bottom of the downspout.

00:03:35.430 --> 00:03:37.150
Depending on the type of tree

00:03:37.150 --> 00:03:40.440
and location of landscaping
around your home

00:03:40.440 --> 00:03:41.965
will determine how often

00:03:41.965 --> 00:03:43.456
you should clean your gutters out

00:03:43.456 --> 00:03:46.160
and what type of debris may fall in them.

00:03:46.160 --> 00:03:47.843
A good practice would be

00:03:47.843 --> 00:03:50.209
to trim back trees as needed

00:03:50.209 --> 00:03:53.420
and plant future trees
further away from the home.

